        "minutes_note": "{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\deff0\\deflang1033{\\fonttbl{\\f0\\fnil\\fcharset0 Arial;}{\\f1\\fnil Arial;}}\r\n{\\colortbl ;\\red0\\green0\\blue0;}\r\n\\viewkind4\\uc1\\pard\\cf1\\ul\\fs24 ACTION 1:\\par\r\n\\ulnone By unanimous consent, the Council Rules were suspended to allow Tamaso Johnson, Council Central Staff, to address the Council.\\ul\\par\r\n\\par\r\nACTION 2:\\ulnone\\par\r\nCouncilmember Rivera announced she was disqualifying herself from voting on Council Bill 121000 due to a conflict of interest.\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\ul ACTION 3:\\ulnone\\par\r\nMotion was made by Councilmember Saka and duly seconded to amend Council Bill 121000, by adding a new Section 3 and renumbering the remaining sections accordingly, as shown in the underlined and strike through language below:\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\ul Section 3. The Council requests that the Seattle Department of Construction and Inspections (SDCI): (1) conduct outreach efforts to educate landlords about the requirements of this ordinance; and (2) transmit to Council, by January 31, 2026, a report describing the results of these outreach efforts and proposing potential ways that SDCI could assist with the implementation of this ordinance.\\ulnone\\f1\\par\r\n\\f0\\par\r\n\\par\r\nThe motion carried by the following vote:\\par\r\nIn favor: 7 - Hollingsworth, Kettle, Moore, Nelson, Rinck, Saka, Solomon\\par\r\nOpposed: None\\par\r\nDisqualified: 1 - Rivera\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\ul ACTION 4:\\ulnone\\f1\\par\r\n\\f0 Motion was made by Councilmember Moore and duly seconded to amend Section 1 of Council Bill 121000 as shown in Attachment 1 to the Minutes.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nThe motion carried by the following vote:\\par\r\nIn favor: 7 - Hollingsworth, Kettle, Moore, Nelson, Rinck, Saka, Solomon\\par\r\nOpposed: None\\par\r\nDisqualified: 1 - Rivera\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\ul ACTION 5:\\ulnone\\par\r\nMotion was made by Councilmember Rinck and duly seconded to amend Section 1 of Council Bill 121000 as shown in the underlined language below:\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\b 7.34.040 Enforcement and penalties\\b0\\par\r\nThe City Attorney may file a civil action in a court of competent jurisdiction for violations of this Chapter 7.34 for civil penalties of up to $7,500 per violation. \\ul Each instance of coordinating services for each dwelling unit may be considered a separate violation.\\ulnone  The court may award reasonable attorneys\\rquote  fees and costs to the City Attorney if the City Attorney is the prevailing party.\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\b 7.34.050 Private right of action\\b0\\par\r\nAny person injured by a violation of this Chapter 7.34 may bring a civil action in a court of competent jurisdiction against a landlord or other person violating this Chapter 7.34 to recover damages up to $7,500 per violation \\ul in addition to any actual damages incurred\\ulnone . The court may award reasonable attorneys\\rquote  fees and costs to any person injured by a violation of this Chapter 7.34 if that person is the prevailing party.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nThe motion carried by the following vote:\\par\r\nIn favor: 6 - Hollingsworth, Kettle, Moore, Nelson, Rinck, Solomon\\par\r\nOpposed: 1 - Saka\\par\r\nDisqualified: 1 - Rivera\\par\r\n}\r\n",
